From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tim Besard <tim.besard@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 09:09:40 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 43/46] Preserve SPIR struct layouts when retargeting SPIR
 modules

Before linking a SPIR module with the kernel library, its datalayout is
replaced by the host's. Struct types from SPIR(-V) aren't padded explicitly,
because the SPIR datalayout already aligns long and double to 8 bytes as
OpenCL C requires. Where the host aligns them less (i386), field offsets,
struct sizes and array strides silently changed, while sizeof and offsetof,
folded by the SPIR compiler, still reported the SPIR layout.

Before the swap, replace every struct type whose layout differs, wherever it
describes memory, by a packed struct with explicit padding: GEPs (renumbering
the field indices), allocas, globals and their initializers, and the
byval/sret/byref/elementtype attributes. Aggregate SSA values have no
layout and keep their type; aggregate loads and stores are split into
per-element accesses at the SPIR offsets. This is what Clang does for
OpenCL C on i386, and a no-op where the layouts agree (all 64-bit hosts).

The test checks struct buffers, by-value struct arguments, nested structs,
tail padding, private arrays of structs, constant initializers and aggregate
loads and stores, from 32-bit and 64-bit SPIR-V.

(cherry picked from commit 3a901f268a3f42a20f6e5374c0e25aa76f1c3ac9)
